, {"definition": "(figurative) To terminate an official sitting; to adjourn.", "tags": ["figuratively", "intransitive"], "examples": ["The committee rose after agreeing to the report."], "quotes": [{"text": "It was near nine[…]before the House rose.", "reference": "1849–1861, Thomas Babington Macaulay, chapter X, in The History of England from the Accession of James the Second, volume (please specify |volume=I to V), London: Longman, Brown, Green, and Longmans, →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}]}, {"definition": "(intransitive) To increase in value or standing.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": [{"definition": "To attain a higher status.", "tags": ["intransitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Some rise by sin, and some by virtue fall.", "reference": "c. 1603–1604 (date written), William Shakespeare, “Measure for Measure”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act II, scene i]:"}, {"text": "among the rising theologians of Germany", "reference": "1846, Julius Hare, The Mission of the Comforter:"}, {"text": "Caesar Enrico “Rico” Bandello is a hoodlum who rises swiftly in Chicago’s mob world, in the 1931 film “Little Caesar.” Played by Edward G. Robinson, Rico is tracked down by police and shot.", "reference": "2023 August 20, Richard Galant, “Opinion: A powerful weapon for prosecutors is now in the hands of Fani Willis”, in CNN:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "Of a quantity, price, etc., to increase.", "tags": ["intransitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Investors face a quandary. Cash offers a return of virtually zero in many developed countries; government-bond yields may have risen in recent weeks but they are still unattractive. Equities have suffered two big bear markets since 2000 and are wobbling again. It is hardly surprising that pension funds, insurers and endowments are searching for new sources of return.", "reference": "2013 July 6, “The rise of smart beta”, in The Economist, volume 408, number 8843, page 68:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "To become more and more dignified or forcible; to increase in interest or power; said of style, thought, or discourse.", "tags": ["intransitive"], "examples": ["to rise in force of expression; to rise in eloquence; a story rises in interest."], "quotes": [{"text": "The humor of my proposition appealed more strongly to Miss Trevor than I had looked for, and from that time forward she became her old self again;[…]. Our table in the dining-room became again the abode of scintillating wit and caustic repartee, Farrar bracing up to his old standard, and the demand for seats in the vicinity rose to an animated competition.", "reference": "1897 December (indicated as 1898), Winston Churchill, chapter VIII, in The Celebrity: An Episode, New York, N.Y.: The Macmillan Company; London: Macmillan & Co., Ltd., →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(music) To ascend on a musical scale; to take a higher pitch.", "tags": ["intransitive"], "examples": ["to rise a tone or semitone"], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}]}, {"definition": "(intransitive) To begin, to develop; to be initiated.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": [{"definition": "To become active, effective or operational, especially in response to an external or internal stimulus.", "tags": ["intransitive"], "examples": ["to rise to the occasion", "Thus far, my intellect has been able to rise sufficiently to meet every academic challenge that I have encountered.", "As Patrick continued to goad me, I felt my temper rising towards the limits of my self control."], "quotes": [{"text": "Professor Peter Crome, chair of the audit's steering group, said the report \"provides further concrete evidence that the care of patients with dementia in hospital is in need of a radical shake-up\". While a few hospitals had risen to the challenge of improving patients' experiences, many have not, he said. The report recommends that all staff receive basic dementia awareness training, and staffing levels should be maintained to help such patients.", "reference": "2011 December 16, Denis Campbell, “Hospital staff 'lack skills to cope with dementia patients'”, in Guardian:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "To develop, to come about or intensify.", "tags": ["intransitive"], "examples": ["As hunger and despondency became more intense, a determination rose within me to find a way of getting off the desert island."], "quotes": [{"text": "To-night the winds begin to rise\n⁠And roar from yonder dropping day:\n⁠The last red leaf is whirl’d away,\nThe rooks are blown about the skies; […]", "reference": "1850, [Alfred, Lord Tennyson], “Canto XV”, in In Memoriam, London: Edward Moxon, […], →OCLC, page 24:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "To swell or puff up in the process of fermentation; to become light.", "tags": ["intransitive"], "examples": ["Has that dough risen yet?"], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(of a river) To have its source (in a particular place).", "tags": ["intransitive"], "examples": ["The majestic Marannon, or Amazon River, rises out of the Lake Launcocha, situated in the province of Tarma, in 10° 14ʹ south latitude, and ten leagues to the north of Pasco."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "bg", "name": "Bulgarian"}, "word": "възниквам"}, {